Here's a C++ function that takes a reference to a `std::string` containing markdown formatted text and returns a `std::string` containing HTML formatted text:

```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <string>
#include <sstream>
#include <regex>

std::string markdownToHtml(const std::string& markdown) {
    std::string html;
    std::regex regex(R"(#(\S+))");
    std::string match;

    while (std::regex_search(markdown, match, regex)) {
        std::string level = match[1];
        html += "<h" + level + ">" + match + "</h" + level + ">\n";
    }

    return html;
}

int main() {
    std::string markdown = "# Main Heading\n## Subheading\n### Subheading";
    std::string html = markdownToHtml(markdown);
    std::cout << html << std::endl;
    return 0;
}
```

This function uses a regular expression to match markdown headings and converts them to HTML headings. The regular expression `R(#(\S+))` matches a sequence of non-space characters preceded by a `#` symbol, which represents the heading level. The function then constructs the HTML string by replacing each markdown heading with the corresponding HTML heading.

